WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
More Australians die of cancer each year now

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.320
than thirty years ago.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.680
Yet after allowing for the changing age mix,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.880
the cancer death rate is lower.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.160
Both statements are true.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.360
To see how, you need to know exactly

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.760
what each disease number measures.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.800
So today's question: what do incidence, prevalence and

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.160
mortality measure?

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.486
You've met two of these with dengue.

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.806
Incidence counts new cases in a set time,

00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:31.606
and prevalence counts everyone who has the disease

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:32.966
at one moment.

00:00:33.287 --> 00:00:36.686
Incidence is the tap, prevalence is the tank.

00:00:37.047 --> 00:00:38.366
Now add a third.

00:00:38.647 --> 00:00:41.126
Mortality counts deaths from the disease in a

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:44.406
set time, mort as in mortuary.

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:46.966
So it's how many new cases, how many

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:49.846
people living with it, and how many deaths.

00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:53.626
Every rate needs three things defined.

00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:56.906
First, what counts as a case, for example

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.426
a diagnosis confirmed by a doctor.

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.906
Second, the population at risk: the people who

00:01:02.987 --> 00:01:04.306
could get the disease.

00:01:04.507 --> 00:01:07.506
For cervical cancer, that's people with a cervix.

00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:10.986
Third, the time window: usually a year for

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.706
incidence and mortality, but a single date for

00:01:13.787 --> 00:01:16.106
prevalence, because it's a snapshot.

00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:18.506
Leave any of these vague, and two rates

00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:20.906
that look alike may not be comparable.

00:01:22.134 --> 00:01:24.853
Let's work all three for type 2 diabetes

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:27.253
in one city of eighty thousand people.

00:01:27.814 --> 00:01:30.693
In 2025, a hundred and sixty people were

00:01:30.774 --> 00:01:32.093
newly diagnosed.

00:01:32.374 --> 00:01:35.013
For incidence, new cases go on top and

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:38.373
the population goes underneath, then we multiply by

00:01:38.454 --> 00:01:39.973
a hundred thousand.

00:01:40.054 --> 00:01:42.453
A hundred and sixty divided by eighty thousand

00:01:42.694 --> 00:01:45.333
is zero point zero zero two.

00:01:45.454 --> 00:01:48.213
Times a hundred thousand, that's two hundred per

00:01:48.294 --> 00:01:49.893
hundred thousand per year.

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:54.133
Sense check: that's two in every thousand people,

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:54.933
which fits.

00:01:56.134 --> 00:01:57.253
Now prevalence.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:01.173
On the thirtieth of June 2025, four thousand

00:02:01.414 --> 00:02:03.413
four hundred people in the city were living

00:02:03.494 --> 00:02:05.013
with type 2 diabetes.

00:02:05.654 --> 00:02:08.453
These are all existing cases, counted on one

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:10.693
day, so there's no "per year".

00:02:11.574 --> 00:02:14.613
Four thousand four hundred over eighty thousand is

00:02:14.694 --> 00:02:17.333
zero point zero five five.

00:02:17.374 --> 00:02:19.733
As a percentage, that's five point five per

00:02:19.814 --> 00:02:20.693
cent.

00:02:20.774 --> 00:02:24.373
Per hundred thousand, it's five thousand five hundred.

00:02:24.494 --> 00:02:26.933
Use whichever form the question asks for, but

00:02:27.014 --> 00:02:27.973
don't mix them.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.800
Third, mortality.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.800
In 2025, sixteen people in the city died

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.320
from type 2 diabetes.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.880
Sixteen over eighty thousand, times a hundred thousand,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.520
is twenty per hundred thousand per year.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.280
Notice the whole population goes underneath, not just

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.160
the people who had the disease.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.440
That's what makes it a mortality rate.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
The share of patients who die is a

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:54.880
different measure.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.620
Side by side: two hundred new cases, five

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.260
thousand five hundred living with it, and twenty

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.020
deaths, all per hundred thousand.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.340
Prevalence is far bigger because type 2 diabetes

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.140
usually lasts for life, so the tank keeps

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.220
filling and little drains out.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.620
Pancreatic cancer is the opposite.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.100
Few people get it, but most die within

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.740
a few years, so prevalence stays low.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.860
And better treatment that keeps people alive longer

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.380
raises prevalence, even when incidence doesn't change.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.680
Now the hook.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.120
If a town doubles in size and its

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.840
cancer cases double too, the rate hasn't changed.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.240
But a rate fixes size, not age, and

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.560
most cancers are far more common in older

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:42.600
people.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.720
Australia's population is bigger and older than thirty

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:46.840
years ago.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.160
So the count of cancer deaths has risen,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.120
and the unadjusted, or crude, rate has barely

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.400
moved.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.200
An age-standardised rate works out what the rate

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.280
would be if both years had the same

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.560
age mix.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.880
On that measure, deaths have fallen.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.820
To collect these data, use a trusted source

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.980
such as the Australian Institute of Health and

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.140
Welfare, and check each figure's denominator.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.820
If one value's a percentage and another's per

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.100
hundred thousand, convert before you compare.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.060
Then graph them: a line graph for change

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.020
over time, a bar graph for comparing groups,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.940
with the units on the axis, and the

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.860
source and year in the caption.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.660
And remember, a rise in incidence can come

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.780
from better screening, not only more disease.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.360
Two exam questions.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:36.400
First.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.320
In 2024, Region X, with one point two

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.600
million people, recorded five hundred and forty new

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.880
lung cancer cases.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.800
Region Y, with three hundred thousand people, recorded

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.560
one hundred and eighty.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.360
Calculate the incidence in each region per hundred

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.640
thousand.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.600
Region Y's population is much older.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.480
Explain why these rates can't show which region's

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.640
people are at greater risk.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.000
Pause here to answer.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.120
Region X is five hundred and forty over

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.400
one point two million, times a hundred thousand:

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.160
forty-five per hundred thousand per year.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.360
Region Y is sixty.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.760
But lung cancer is much more common in

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.280
older people, so Y's older population could explain

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.040
its higher crude rate.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.200
To compare risk fairly, you'd need age-standardised rates.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.680
The classic mistake is saying X is worse

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.440
because it had more cases.

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:43.626
Second.

00:05:43.707 --> 00:05:47.226
The table shows incidence, mortality and prevalence for

00:05:47.307 --> 00:05:51.466
a non-infectious disease in Australia in 2005, 2015

00:05:51.547 --> 00:05:55.786
and 2025, each per hundred thousand people.

00:05:56.507 --> 00:05:58.666
Describe the trend in each measure.

00:05:58.987 --> 00:06:01.626
Then suggest an explanation for the rise in

00:06:01.707 --> 00:06:03.626
prevalence that fits the table.

00:06:03.947 --> 00:06:05.306
Pause here to answer.

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:18.613
Incidence stayed steady at about sixty new cases

00:06:18.694 --> 00:06:19.813
per hundred thousand.

00:06:20.294 --> 00:06:23.413
Mortality halved, from thirty to fifteen.

00:06:23.974 --> 00:06:26.533
Prevalence rose from three hundred to five hundred

00:06:26.654 --> 00:06:27.733
and forty.

00:06:27.774 --> 00:06:30.213
The pattern is consistent with people living longer

00:06:30.294 --> 00:06:33.413
with the disease, so existing cases build up

00:06:33.494 --> 00:06:35.813
while new-case rates stay similar.

00:06:35.894 --> 00:06:38.533
The classic mistake is saying more people are

00:06:38.694 --> 00:06:39.733
getting the disease.

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:44.253
So for any disease data, ask three things.

00:06:44.454 --> 00:06:47.093
Is it new cases, people living with it,

00:06:47.174 --> 00:06:48.093
or deaths?

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:50.693
Over what population and time?

00:06:50.854 --> 00:06:53.173
And is it a count, a crude rate

00:06:53.414 --> 00:06:55.573
or an age-standardised rate?

00:06:55.694 --> 00:06:59.053
Put everything on one denominator before you compare.

00:06:59.494 --> 00:07:03.413
Next, practice with new data: calculating rates and

00:07:03.494 --> 00:07:04.773
judging what they show.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.440
Here are the key notes.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.600
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.480
to copy them.
